<!--
	Maps and the Map Device (MapDeviceData, MapDeviceManager; settings in config/Custom/MapDevice.ini).

	hideout     where a player arrives in their hideout (.hideout): x, y, z, heading. It is a private copy (instance) of the place.
	            Default: the cabin of the Jinia Guild Hideout, an instance-only room (about 560 x 800).
	  device    where the Map Device stands; its portals open in a ring round it (PortalRadius in the ini).

	difficulty  how many tiers higher a map stronger monsters drop, on top of the tier of their level:
	            champion1/2/3 a Champion of that tier, special a Mage, Thief, Infused, Resonant or wave challenge monster, raid a raid boss.

	strength    how a monster's strength score is worked out (MonsterStrength). Its effective stats (the npc data's, times its passive skills such as
	            "HP Increase (3x)" or "Strong P. Def.") are compared with the standard monster of its level (the lower quartile of each stat over every
	            monster of the level, which is the ordinary monster of that level): 2.0 is twice the standard.
	            The score is the weighted geometric mean of those ratios: 1.0 is an ordinary monster, a Spiked Stakato 2.0, a Maluk Leader 6.1, Knoriks 10.3.
	            The attributes are the weights: hp, pAtk, mAtk, pDef, mDef, pAtkSpd (0 leaves a stat out). HP counts twice, so toughness
	            (HP, P. Def., M. Def.) and offence (P. Atk., M. Atk., Atk. Spd.) weigh about the same. Casting speed is not scored: every npc casts at 333.

	tier        a map tier: tier (1 = lowest), itemId (its "Map (Tier N)" item),
	            minLevel/maxLevel the levels of the monsters of its map, minScore/maxScore their strength score (maxScore 0 = no limit). A map is filled
	            with plain open world monsters that match both (a respawning spawn, they move and give experience: no quest, event or raid monster).
	            If no monster matches, the tier takes the closest in strength of its levels and the server log says so (//mapdevice pool <tier> lists them).
	            dropLevel the lowest monster level that drops this tier; the monster's score must also reach minScore (a monster drops the highest
	            tier both allow, plus its difficulty), so a monster drops the tier it would fill (ordinary monsters never drop the party tiers).
	            hp/attack/defence how much stronger the monsters of its map are, reward the experience, SP and drop chance multiplier of its map.
	            Tiers 1-5 hold solo monsters, tiers 6-10 party monsters: their own strength makes them harder, so their multipliers stay near 1.

	arena       a field a map can open in, picked at random: name, the middle x, y and the ground z, the fence's radiusX/radiusY (an ellipse,
	            at least 500), entryAngle where the players come in (degrees round the middle, 0 = east, 90 = south; the entry is at 80% of the radius).
	            The defaults were checked against the geodata: open ground, at least 95% of it walkable from the middle. They are twice the radius
	            (four times the area) of the first fields, so a map's monsters stand well apart (MonsterSpacing in the ini).
	            A map is its own copy of the place, without its world monsters.

	excludedNpcs monster ids that never fill a map, comma separated.

	uniqueMap   a unique map (UniqueMapManager): an item that always opens the same map, with the modifiers it names instead of random ones, no medal
	            and no hot zone effect, a twist of its own while it is cleared and a final encounter once it is (both worked by type, see
	            docs/map-device.md). Unique maps only drop inside maps: on completing a map of a tier they drop at (dropMinTier-dropMaxTier),
	            and from the unique bosses themselves (config/Custom/MapDevice.ini).
	  type      COLISEUM, VAULT, OSSUARY, DEN, CRUCIBLE, FRONT or TOMB: which twist and final encounter it has.
	  itemId    the item that opens it (data/stats/items/custom/map_device_items.xml).
	  tier      the tier whose monsters fill it (their levels and strength) and whose multipliers they get.
	  monsters  how many monsters it holds before the final encounter.
	  arena     the arena of this file (or the id of an event map) it opens in; any of the tier's if unknown. An arena of this file is shrunk
	            round its middle for it, so its fewer monsters stand UniqueMapDensity times as densely as a normal map's (MapDevice.ini).
	  modifiers the map's modifiers (MapModifier names, see docs/map-device.md).
	  npcs      optional: the monsters that fill it (type="Monster" npc ids, comma separated), all of them, instead of 6 kinds of the tier's.
	  dropMinTier, dropMaxTier  optional: the tiers of map whose completion may drop it (default UniqueMapMinTier of MapDevice.ini to 10).
	            The boss of a unique map drops another unique map of its own tier's.
	  reward    experience, SP and drop chance multiplier, on top of the tier's.
	  boss      the plain monster (type="Monster") its boss is made from; bossName its title; bossHp/bossAttack/bossDefence multiply its stats
	            on top of the map's.
	  drop      group="map": every monster of the map may drop it (half as often from a copy or a minion); group="boss": the boss drops it;
	            group="special": the twist's own monsters drop it (the Coliseum's Challengers, the Vault's spilled coins, the Ossuary's Echoes).
	            id, min, max, chance in percent. The Vault's Greed raises the chance of its map and boss drops.
-->
